#{[17592186105582 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y = \\ln(x-2).$\n\n![](images/bf/bfe277aaf4c57d73d2649d8ade756cf04411ba23a049a6e1283d18c3e711a7fa.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\leq 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 2^{-}} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 2^{+}} \\ln (x-2) = -\\infty.\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 2} \\ln (x-2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186105583 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2)\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2).\n$$"] [17592186105583 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y = \\ln(x+2).$\n\n![](images/90/906449fdcfacb0dc4aabcd51d363a71ca4842fd1a70c4b8537d38fe1f52ef7d8.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\leq -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to (-2)^{-}} \\ln (x+2) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x\\gt -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to (-2)^{+}} \\ln (x+2) = -\\infty.\n$$"] [17592186105608 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty, \\infty),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our integrals.\n\n- Computing the integral in statement I, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n \\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{-x}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n&=  \\lim_{a \\to \\infty} \\int_{0}^{a} e^{-x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\\n&=  \\lim_{a \\to \\infty} \\left[-e^{-x}\\right]_0^a\\\\\n&=  -\\lim_{a \\to \\infty}  \\left({e^{-a}}-1\\right)\\\\\n&=  1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges, and statement I is true.\n\n- Computing the integral in statement II, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{e}^{-x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-a}-1) \\\\  & =+\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral diverges, and statement II is true.\n\n- The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx= \\int_{-\\infty}^0 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx + \\int_0^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nHowever, the first integral on the right-hand side is divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent, and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are all true."] [17592186105626 :question/worked-solution "Consider a cross-section at some point $x$, where $x \\in \\big(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}\\big)$, and its projection onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/90/9020b30f51b2b4834fb0a587ae62c5b8f3c3fe7006fae81927d8a2fdcde2d82d.png)\n\nThen, the base of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|y|=|2-{x}^{2}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the diameter $d$ of a semicircle, the corresponding area can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{4}\\pi d^2 = \\dfrac{1}{8}\\pi d^2\n$$\n\nSo, the expression for the area of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(x) &= \\frac{1}{8}πd^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8} \\mid 2 - \\left.x^{2}\\right|^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8}(4 - 4x^{2} + x^{4})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the volume of the solid, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\int_{a}^{b}A(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8}\\int_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4 - 4x^{2} + x^{4})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105642 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the set of numbers between $1$ and $100$ that are divisible by $3$, and let $B$ be the set of numbers that are divisible by $5$. Then,\n\n- $A\\cap B$ is the set of numbers that are divisible by both $3$ and $5$, and\n- $A \\cup B$ is the set of numbers that are divisible by $3$ or $5$.\n\nWe want to compute $\\mid A \\cup B \\mid$, and we can do so using the inclusion-exclusion principle:\n\n$$\n|A\\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A\\cap B|\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\mid A \\mid$ by counting the number of multiples of $3$ that are less than or equal to $100{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A &= \\left\\lfloor \\dfrac{100}{3}\\right\\rfloor = \\lfloor 33.3...\\rfloor =33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n(Recall that the floor function $\\lfloor x \\rfloor$ returns the greatest integer that is smaller than or equal to $x$.)\n\nWe compute $\\mid B \\mid$ in the same way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid B &= \\left\\lfloor \\dfrac{100}{5}\\right\\rfloor = \\lfloor 20 \\rfloor =20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $A\\cap B$ is the set of numbers that are divisible by both $3$ and $5$. Since $3$ and $5$ do not have any common factors, any number that is divisible by both $3$ and $5$ must be divisible by\n\n$$\n3 \\times 5 = 15\n$$\n\nSo, we can compute $\\mid A \\cap B \\mid$ in the usual way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A\\cap B &= \\left\\lfloor \\dfrac{100}{15}\\right\\rfloor = \\lfloor 6.66 \\rfloor =6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inclusion-exclusion principle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A\\cup B| & =|A|+|B|-|A\\cap B| \\\\  & =33+20-6 \\\\  & =47.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that there are $47$ integers between $1$ and $100$ that are divisible by $3$ or $5$."] [17592186105643 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ be the set of ways we can distribute the balls if there are no restrictions. Then $\\mid X \\mid$ is the number of ways to place $11$ objects into $2$ categories, which we can compute as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle | X | & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11 + 2 - 1}{11} \\right) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{11} \\right) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{12 !}{11 ! 1 !}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 12.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the set of ways to distribute the balls where we leave at most one ball in the first box, and let $B$ be the set of ways to distribute the balls leaving at most one ball in the second box. Then $A \\cup B$ is the set of ways to distribute the balls such that some box contains at most one ball, and we can compute the desired quantity as\n\n$$\n|X| - |A \\cup B|.\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\mid A \\cup B \\mid$, we can use the inclusion-exclusion principle.\n\n- Note that $\\mid A \\mid = 2$ since there are only two ways to distribute the balls such that the first box contains at most one ball (either no balls or one ball in the first box). By the same reasoning, we also have $\\mid B|=2$.\n- Also, note that $A\\cap B$ has no elements since it corresponds to the situation when both boxes are empty or each box has at most one ball (remember that we distribute $11$ balls in total). In other words, $\\mid A\\cap B|=0$.\n\nSo, according to the inclusion-exclusion principle, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A\\cup B| & =|A|+|B|-|A\\cap B| \\\\  & =2+2-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of ways to distribute the balls into the two boxes such that each box contains at least two balls is given by\n\n$$\n|X|-|A\\cup B|=12-4=8.\n$$"] [17592186105644 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the set of permutations with the letter \"$a$\" in the first or second position, and let $B$ be the set of permutations with the letter \"$b$\" in the first or second position. Then the set $A\\cup B$ is the set of permutations that have a letter in the first or second position.\n\nWe want to compute $\\mid A \\cup B \\mid$, and we can do so using the inclusion-exclusion principle:\n\n$$\n|A\\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A\\cap B|\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\mid A \\mid$, note that we have $3$! permutations with \"$a$\" in the first position and $3$! permutations with \"$a$\" in the second position. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A &= 3!+3!=6+6=12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy similar reasoning, we also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid B &= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\mid A \\cap B \\mid$, first note that $A \\cap B$ is the set of permutations that have both \"$a$\" and \"$b$\" in the first and second position ($ab$XX or $ba$XX). For each of these possibilities, there are $2$! ways to order the remaining elements (the two numbers). So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A\\cap B &= 2!+2!=2+2=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inclusion-exclusion principle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A\\cup B| & =|A|+|B|-|A\\cap B| \\\\  & =12+12-4 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $20$ permutations that have a letter in the first or second position."] [17592186106052 :question/problem "![](images/f2/f299f0d25bfee9303a6485413d44f1a6ce7b863c79ccae91f4f8d5d604f2bac3.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{x-1}{4},\\, & 0\\le x<4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186106054 :question/problem "![](images/88/8835c085ca34d59f5c79786e12ed67cb33ccfdcfefd706dcc83ca2be0239670f.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}3{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186106090 :question/worked-solution "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c|c}x & (-\\infty ,0) & 0 & (0,2) & 2 & (2,\\infty ) \\\\ \\hline f & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ \\hline {f}^{′} & + & 0 & - & 0 & +\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ on the interval $(0,2).$"] [17592186106282 :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=-{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=-2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{-{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106285 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{5}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-\\frac{1}{4}$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5} & =-\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4}\\right)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186106286 :question/problem "Given that $p(x) =-5x^{8}$ and $q(x) = \\frac{1}{15x^{2}}$, what is $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}(p(x) \\cdot q(x))$?"] [17592186106287 :question/problem "Given that $p(x) =-\\frac{2}{3x^{4}}$ and $q(x) =-\\frac{1}{6x^{3}}$, what is $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}(x \\cdot p(x))/(q(x))$?"] [17592186106288 :question/worked-solution "Applying the power rule and the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{(xf(x))}^{3} & =(\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3})\\cdot (\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)^{3}) \\\\  & =(\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3})\\cdot {(\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x))}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{(xf(x))}^{3} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {(3)}^{3} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =27\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{3}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $27$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{(xf(x))}^{3}=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186106289 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}(-7x^{6})$."] [17592186106303 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}(6x^{3} - x + 6)$."] [17592186106377 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is convergent” is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }({x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }({a}^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that ${a}^{3}\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({x}^{3}{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({0}^{3}-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that ${a}^{3}\\to -\\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is true. The integral in statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” and “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” are true."] [17592186106440 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  2^{+}}\\frac{x^{2} + 2x - 3}{10x - 20}$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106441 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  (-3)^{+}}\\frac{x^{2} + 4}{3x^{2} - 27}$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106442 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  4^{-}}(- (1)/(x(x - 4)))$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106443 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  (-1)^{-}}\\frac{x}{2x^{2} - 2}$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106445 :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\frac{x + 3}{x^{2} - 9}$, which of the following statements is correct?\n\n1. $\\lim_{x \\to  (-3)^{-}}f(x) = - ∞$\n2. $\\lim_{x \\to  (-3)^{+}}f(x) = + ∞$\n3. $\\lim_{x \\to  3^{+}}f(x) = ∞$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106448 :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\frac{2x + 2}{x^{2} - 1}$, which of the following statements is correct?\n\n1. $\\lim_{x \\to  1^{-}}f(x) = - ∞$\n2. $\\lim_{x \\to  (-1)^{+}}f(x) = + ∞$\n3. $\\lim_{x \\to  (-1)^{-}}f(x) = - ∞$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106450 :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (x^{2})/((x - 1)^{3})$, which of the following statements is correct?\n\n1. $\\lim_{x \\to  1^{-}}f(x) = + ∞$\n2. $\\lim_{x \\to  1}f(x) = + ∞$\n3. $\\lim_{x \\to  1^{+}}f(x) = + ∞$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106451 :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (2x)/((x - 2)^{2})$, which of the following statements is correct?\n\n1. $\\lim_{x \\to  2^{-}}f(x) = + ∞$\n2. $\\lim_{x \\to  2}f(x) = + ∞$\n3. $\\lim_{x \\to  2^{+}}f(x) = - ∞$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186106452 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}(5 + 2x - 4x^{3} + 6x^{2})$."] [17592186106453 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  (-4)}\\log_{2} (x + 4)$."] [17592186106454 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  1/2}\\ln (2x - 1)$."] [17592186106455 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  (-3)^{+}}\\ln (x + 3)$."] [17592186106456 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}\\log_{2} (5 - x)$?"] [17592186106457 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}\\log (2x - 4)$?"] [17592186106458 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  7}\\log (15x - 5)$?"] [17592186106459 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  5}\\log_{3} (5x + 2)$?"] [17592186106460 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  5^{-}}\\log (5 - x)$?"] [17592186106466 :question/problem "Given that $p(x) =-2x^{5}$ and $q(x) = 4x^{5}$, what is $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}(p(x))/(q(x))$?"] [17592186106492 :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{3}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106499 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{2}, & x=3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}, & x=7\\end{cases}\\hspace{2em}g(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{3}, & x=3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}, & x=7\\end{cases}\\hspace{2em}h(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{3}{2}, & x=3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}, & x=7\\end{cases}\\hspace{2em}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186107001 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the base of a solid is the region enclosed by the line\n\n$$\ny=-2x+2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 1\n$$\n\nas shown below. If cross-sections perpendicular to the $x$-axis are semicircles, how do we calculate the volume of the solid?\n\n![](images/04/04e6da85e925bbb49f9d1e1e67fb08ecf932fa9dcda7bd0a376bb16ac13d73d2.png)\n\nA sketch with a few cross-sections is shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/0a/0af3b8f2977733dcd444507a14876674cdcd6d1fb1792cc523ea332cc56cfb1b.png)\n\nConsider a cross-section at some point $x$, where $x \\in (0,1)$, and its projection onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/90/90972f47117425f9785c65b459afbbc4d82c5171841314768fbca03aeee34fb9.png)\n\nIf we project the rectangular cross-section onto the $xy$-plane, we get the picture below:\n\n![](images/89/892c744de907e9440608a3f00a1b871dcb444458636b814bd3b62a2cf104b873.png)\n\nThen, the base of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|y|=|-2x+2|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the diameter $d$ of a semicircle, we can find the corresponding area using the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\overbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{4}\\pi d^2}_{\\textrm{Full circle}}}^{\\textrm{Semicircle}} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\\pi d^2\n$$\n\nSo, the expression for the area of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(x) &= \\frac{1}{8}πd^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8} \\mid - 2x + \\left.2\\right|^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8}(4x^{2} - 8x + 4) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2}(1 - 2x + x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the volume of the solid, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\int_{a}^{b}A(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{π}{2}(1 - 2x + x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2}\\int_{0}^{1}(1 - 2x + x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{π}{2}(x - x^{2} + \\frac{x^{3}}{3})\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2}[(1 - 1 + \\frac{1}{3}) - 0] \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186107006 :tutorial/content "Now, let's suppose that the base of a solid is the region below enclosed by the line\n\n$$\nx=y+2\n$$\n\nand the $y$-axis for\n\n$$\n-2 \\leq y \\leq 0\n$$\n\nGiven that cross-sections perpendicular to the $y$-axis are semicircles, how do we calculate the volume of the solid?\n\n![](images/e9/e91f3e159db520cf904203df32e3494034e3f66b9125c8acf437b6bed44a2de7.png)\n\nA sketch with a few cross-sections is shown in the picture below:\n\n![](images/a7/a7b5c077e0e3a278dbe0ba0e2be225bac92d95e5c0a594389c58dbb516245dc5.png)\n\nNow, consider the picture with only one cross-section that passes through some point $y \\in (-2,0)$:\n\n![](images/af/af52e5519324f5115fcdbd78df6b93074c2e595fa240d9e1fec0082a8dccdcbc.png)\n\nThe projection of the previous picture on the $xy$-plane is given below:\n\n![](images/69/69179521f03a14aa52f74bb15f382fdafacd30d65fb9f49810126fb5127063f5.png)\n\nThen, the base of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|y|=|y+2|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the diameter $d$ of a semicircle, we can find the corresponding area using the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{4}\\pi d^2 = \\dfrac{1}{8}\\pi d^2\n$$\n\nSo, the expression for the area of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(y) &= \\frac{1}{8}πd^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8} \\mid y + \\left.2\\right|^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8}(y^{2} + 4y + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the volume of the solid, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\int_{c}^{d}A(y)dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{-2}^{0}\\frac{π}{8}(y^{2} + 4y + 4)dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8}\\int_{-2}^{0}(y^{2} + 4y + 4)dy \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{π}{8}(\\frac{y^{3}}{3} + 2y^{2} + 4y)\\right|_{-2}^{0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8}[0 - (-\\frac{8}{3} + 8 - 8)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186107030 :tutorial/content "Multiplication of a power function by any *positive* real constant does not influence its limit at infinity. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }2{x}^{2} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause $y=2x^2$ is an upward parabola, just like $y=x^2.$\n\n![](images/47/47d8cef2d3d189b32ee1c37f9fa563f58670d0bdc876360b13753106df03a79d.png)\n\nOn the other hand, multiplication of a power function by a *negative* real constant changes the *sign* of the limit. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }-2{x}^{2}=-\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2}=-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause $y=-2x^2$ is a downwards parabola that points in the opposite direction as $y=x^2.$"] [17592186107038 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to \\infty} x^2\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} x^2.\n$$ To do this, we can use the graph of $y=x^2,$ which takes the familiar shape of a parabola.\n\n![](images/58/58536c20f942b37e75a1ebb60fa6298af5fa94d1ba7b2b1cd770a0251796b436.png)\n\nOn the right side of the graph, we see that $y$ increases without bound as $x \\to \\infty.$ On the left side of the graph, we have a similar situation: $y$ again increases without bound as $x \\to -\\infty.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} x^2=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} x^2=\\infty.\n$$\n\nNotice that the functions $y=x^4$ and $y=x^6$ have a similar shape, and as such their limiting behavior is the same.\n\n![](images/5e/5e7386db15731b3486fd64484db69e7919c8e79821d916974f689d737cf23f8a.png)\n\nIn general, whenever $n$ is an *even natural number*, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} x^n=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} x^n=\\infty, \\qquad \\textrm{for}\\qquad n=2,4,6,\\ldots.\n$$"] [17592186107039 :tutorial/content "Now let's look at the graph of $y=x^3.$\n\n![](images/53/53d67c89fce03c6b949aecbe1e55fe8bf912ebadf1c3edc6d6c3601047886b5a.png)\n\nWe notice that the situation here is different:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} x^3=\\infty \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} x^3=-\\infty.\n$$\n\nThese limits are valid for any power function of the form $x^n,$ where $n$ is an *odd natural number*.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} x^n=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} x^n=-\\infty, \\qquad n=1,3,5,\\ldots.\n$$\n\nAgain, multiplication of $x^n$ by a positive constant does not affect the limit at infinity. However, multiplication by a negative constant changes the sign of the limit."] [17592186107040 :tutorial/content "To compute the limit at infinity of a general polynomial, we *only* need to consider the term with the largest exponent, called the **leading term**. All of the other terms can be disregarded!\n\nFor example, to compute \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\left( 6x + 2x^3-7x^2+12\\right) \\, ,\n$$\n\n we identify the leading term and compute its limit. Here, the leading term is ${\\color{blue}{2x^3}},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(6x+2{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+12) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reason this works is that when $x$ becomes very large, the behavior of the polynomial is dominated by the term with the largest exponent.\n\n**Watch out!** The leading term is *not* always the first term that appears in the expression. Rather, it is the term *with the largest exponent*. This term may appear anywhere in the expression.\n\nThe reason why we call the term with the largest exponent the \"leading\" term is that when the polynomial is arranged in standard form (with exponents ordered from greatest to least), the term with the largest exponent appears first:\n\n$$\n6x+ {\\color{blue}{2x^3}}-7x^2+12 \\quad \\to \\quad {\\color{blue}{2x^3}}-7x^2+6x+12\n$$"] [17592186107064 :tutorial/content "Constants do not vary with $x.$ So, any limit of a constant evaluates to just the constant itself.\n\nFor example, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} 3=3.\n$$\n\nLikewise, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} \\sqrt{ \\pi}= \\sqrt{\\pi} .\n$$\n\nIn general, for any constant $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\pm\\infty} C=C.\n$$"] [17592186107093 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the letters $a,b,c,$ and we want to count the number of two-letter permutations that can be formed, where each of the given letters can be repeated.\n\n- We have $3$ choices for the first letter: $a,b,$ or $c.$\n\n- Likewise, we have $3$ choices for the second letter: $a,b,$ or $c.$\n\nThe total number of two-letter permutations that can be formed is given by the product\n\n$$\n3 \\times 3 = 3^2 = 9.\n$$\n\nTo verify that we are correct, we can list out the $9$ permutations below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}aa & ab & ac \\\\ ba & bb & bc \\\\ ca & cb & cc\\end{array}\n$$\n\nIn general, the number of permutations of $k$ items chosen from a set of $n$ with repetition is $n^k.$\n\nTo see why, remember that we have $n$ choices for the first item, $n$ choices for the second item, and so on for all $k$ items. So, by the multiplication principle, the total number of permutations is given by the following product:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{n \\times n \\times \\cdots \\times n}_{\\large k \\textrm{ times}} = n^k\n$$"] [17592186110323 :question/worked-solution "Applying the zero product rule, we get\n\n$$\n2x-1=0\\qquad \\textrm{or}\\qquad 5x+3=0\n$$\n\nWe solve each of the equations separately, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =0 & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & 5x+3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =1 & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & 5x & =-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2} & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & x & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{3}{5},\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$"] [17592186110527 :question/worked-solution "To solve this inequality, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & >2{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{4}-8 & >2{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-8 & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the inequality $P(x) \\gt 2x^2$, we solve the equivalent problem $f(x) \\gt 0$, where\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^4 - 2x^2 - 8\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y = f(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nWe factor the polynomial $f(x)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= x^{4} - 2x^{2} - 8 \\\\\n&= x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 2x^{2} - 8 \\\\\n&= x^{2}(x^{2} - 4) + 2(x^{2} - 4) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 2)(x^{2} - 4) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 2)(x + 2)(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^4 - 2x^2 - 8\n$$\n\nare $x = \\pm{2}$, which are all simple roots.\n\nNext, we graph the curve $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/b6/b65299eb9e1c35485c1af5a1f30c8c6ac14e03e7566a5f562e4e420ccb417a60.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x) \\gt 0$, we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n$x \\in (-\\infty,-2) \\cup (2,\\infty)$."] [17592186111829 :tutorial/content "The **zero product rule** says that if we have the equation\n\n$$\na\\cdot b = 0\n$$\n\nthen $a=0$ or $b=0$.\n\nIn other words, if we know that the product of two numbers is zero, then at least one of them must be zero because any number multiplied by zero is zero.\n\nThe zero product rule is useful for solving quadratic equations. For example, let's take a look at the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(x-1)}{\\color{blue}(x+2)} = 0\n$$\n\nWhenever a quadratic equation is factored on the left-hand side and has zero on the right-hand side, we can apply the zero product rule to solve the equation. So for our equation, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}x-1=0} \\qquad\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\qquad {\\color{blue}x+2 =0}\n$$\n\nWe solve each of the equations separately, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =0 & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & x+2 & =0 \\\\ x & =1 & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x = -2,1$.\n\nAs usual, we can check our solutions by substituting them back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-2 & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & x & =1 \\\\  & ⇓ & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} &  & ⇓ \\\\ (x-1)(x+2) & =0 & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & (x-1)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ (-2-1)(-2+2) & =0 & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & (1-1)(1+2) & =0 \\\\ (-3)(-0) & =0 & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & (0)(3) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} & \\hspace{2em} & \\hspace{2em} & 0 & =0\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186111859 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a rectangle with vertices $A(0,0)$, $B(3,0)$, $C(3,2)$, and $D(0,2)$, as shown below. How can we calculate its area?\n\n![](images/3e/3e10ebebd7f317a5000d5cbdb96864c1d3fcf5161c537fa015db7ce42b1959fe.png)\n\nIf we find the lengths of the sides of the rectangle, then the area $\\mathcal A$ of a rectangle is simply the product of the sides.\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = AB\\cdot BC\n$$\n\nSince $\\overline{AB}$ is horizontal, the length of this segment is simply the absolute value of the difference between the $x$-coordinates of the endpoints:\n\n$$\nAB = |x_B - x_A| = |3-0|=3\n$$\n\nSimilarly, since $\\overline{AD}$ is vertical, the length of this segment is simply the absolute value of the difference between the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints:\n\n$$\nAD = |y_D - y_A| = |2-0| = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of our rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = 3\\cdot 2 = 6\n$$\n\nLet's now take a look at a similar example with a square."] [17592186114944 :question/worked-solution "A *rooted tree* is a tree in which one vertex has been designated the root.\nA *rooted forest* is a graph in which each connected component is a rooted tree.\n\nCayley's formula for the number of labeled trees on $n$ vertices is\n\n$$\n\\mid T_n \\mid = n^{n-2}\n$$\n\nFirst, note that there is a bijection between the set $F_n$ of all labeled rooted forests on $n$ vertices and the set $T_{n+1}$ of all labeled trees on $n+1$ vertices. As a result, these sets have the same cardinality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{F}_{n}| & =|{T}_{n+1}| \\\\  & =(n+1)^{(n+1)-2} \\\\  & =(n+1)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of labeled rooted forests on $9$ vertices is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid F_{9} \\mid &= (9 + 1)^{9 - 1} \\\\\n&= 10^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186114945 :question/problem "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow (-1/2)} x^3.\n$$"] [17592186114945 :question/worked-solution "Substituting\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ndirectly into the limit, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  (-1/2)}x^{3} &= \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{3} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2^{3}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186114955 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\,\\rightarrow \\,-4} \\sqrt{7}.\n$$"] [17592186114955 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-4$, the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt 7\n$$\n\nremains equal to $\\sqrt{7}$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\,\\rightarrow\\, -4} \\sqrt{7}=\\sqrt{7}\n$$"] [17592186114964 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $y=f(x)$. Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,0} \\Bigl(-2.5f(x)\\Bigr).\n$$\n\n![](images/42/42ad0609c570bf56d583811e3a15efd3fa253977738afca69bbc4361ee421216.png)"] [17592186114964 :question/worked-solution "From the graph we get that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}(-2.5f(x)) &= (-2.5) \\cdot lim_(x → 0)f(x) \\\\\n&= (-2.5) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186114966 :question/worked-solution "To find the limit of our vector function, we compute the limit of each component individually:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t \\to 0}\\mathbf f(t) = \\left(\\lim_{t \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-e^t}{\\sin t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i + \\left( \\lim_{t \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin t}{t+t^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j + \\left( \\lim_{t \\to 0} \\dfrac{e^t-e^{-t}}{\\sin t} \\right)\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- The first component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{1-{e}^{t}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{(1-{e}^{t})}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{-{e}^{t}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{0}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t+{t}^{2}} & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t(t+1)} \\\\  & =(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{1}{t+1})\\cdot (\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{0+1}\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{t}-{e}^{-t}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{({e}^{t}-{e}^{-t})}^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{t}+{e}^{-t}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{0}+{e}^{-0}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0}\\mathbf f(t) = -\\mathbf i + \\mathbf j +2\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$"] [17592186114967 :question/worked-solution "To find the limit of our vector function, we compute the limit of each component individually:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-1},\\,\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{3}{t})}^{t},\\,\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-1} & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{t}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{3}{t})}^{t} & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{t/3})}^{t/3}]}^{3} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{t/3})}^{t/3}]}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\,e\\,]^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}} & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-1}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{t \\to \\infty}\\mathbf f(t) = \\left\\langle 0, \\: e^3, \\: -1\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $\\left\\| \\mathbf f(t) \\right\\|$ as $t$ approaches $\\infty$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }‖f(t)‖ & =‖\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖⟨0,\\,{e}^{3},\\,-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+({e}^{3})^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{6}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186114968 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}{e}^{t} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\ {e}^{0} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}1 \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}2t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-2g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)-2\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186114969 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,i+(\\lim_{t\\to 2}t)\\,j+(\\lim_{t\\to 2}{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,i+2\\,j+{2}^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,i+2\\,j+4\\,k, \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 2}g(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to 2}(2-t))\\,i+(\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{6}{t})\\,j+(\\lim_{t\\to 2}(-4))\\,k \\\\  & =(2-2)\\,i+\\frac{6}{2}\\,j+(-4)\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+3\\,j-4\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,j-4\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,i+2\\,j+4\\,k)\\cdot (3\\,j-4\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 3+4\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186114974 :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the limit of $\\mathbf f (t)$ as $t$ approaches $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 0}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}+1),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,{0}^{2}+1,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\rightarrow t_0} \\mathbf f (t) = \\mathbf f (t_0)\n$$ and therefore $\\mathbf f (t)$ is continuous at $t=0.$"] [17592186114975 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf g(1)$ given that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $\\mathbf f(1) = \\left\\langle 1, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\rightarrow 1} \\big[ 2\\mathbf f(t)-\\mathbf g(t) \\big] = \\left\\langle 2, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$"] [17592186114975 :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)-g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 1}2f(t)+\\lim_{t\\to 1}[-g(t)] \\\\  & =2\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)-\\lim_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =2f(1)-g(1) \\\\  & =2\\underbrace{⟨1,1,-1⟩}_{f(1)}-g(1) \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-2⟩-g(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\rightarrow 1} \\big[ 2\\mathbf f(t)-\\mathbf g(t) \\big] = \\left\\langle 2, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $\\mathbf{g} (1)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,2,-2⟩-g(1) & =⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨2,2,-2⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115015 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\pi/8} \\left(1 + 2\\sin \\left(2x\\right) \\right)^2.\n$$"] [17592186115015 :question/worked-solution "Substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\n$$\n\ndirectly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  π/8}(1 + 2\\sin (2x))^{2} &= (1 + 2\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{8}))^{2} \\\\\n&= (1 + 2\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right))^{2} \\\\\n&= (1 + 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= (1 + \\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1^{2} + 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\sqrt{2} + (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 + 2\\sqrt{2} + 2 \\\\\n&= 3 + 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115016 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\sin\\left(2x\\right).\n$$"] [17592186115017 :question/problem "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to \\sqrt{\\pi}/2}\\tan\\left(2x^2-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186115017 :question/worked-solution "Substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\sqrt \\pi}{2}\n$$\n\ndirectly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  \\sqrt{π}/2}\\tan (2x^{2} - \\frac{π}{4}) &= \\tan (2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{π}}{2}\\right)^{2} - \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\tan (2\\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) - \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\tan (\\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115018 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_ {x \\to (-\\pi/6)^{-}} \\tan\\left(x-\\dfrac\\pi 3 \\right).\n$$"] [17592186115018 :question/worked-solution "First, let's try direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  (- π/6)^{-}}\\tan (x - \\frac{π}{3}) &= \\lim_{x \\to  (- π/6)^{-}}(\\sin (x - \\frac{π}{3}))/(\\cos (x - \\frac{π}{3})) \\\\\n&= (\\sin (-\\frac{π}{6} - \\frac{π}{3}))/(\\cos (-\\frac{π}{6} - \\frac{π}{3})) \\\\\n&= (\\sin \\left(-\\frac{π}{2}\\right))/(\\cos \\left(-\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-1}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not defined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\tan\\left(x-\\dfrac\\pi 3 \\right)\n$$\n\n![](images/e2/e201daa486919eaba9f209334ef904a18415f14563da5c85e63047a2d149f3b3.png)\n\nWe see that as $x$ approaches\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 6\n$$\n\nfrom the left, the values of\n\n$$\ny=\\tan\\left(x-\\dfrac\\pi 3 \\right)\n$$\n\nare positive and increase without bound. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_ {x \\to (-\\pi/6)^{-}} \\tan\\left(x-\\dfrac\\pi 3 \\right)= \\infty\n$$"] [17592186115158 :question/worked-solution "The price per day is $\\$50$. So, for $d$ days, Peter will pay $50d$ dollars.\n\nHowever, there is also a flat fee of $\\$70$. So, the total (in dollars) Peter must pay equals\n\n$$\n50d+70\n$$\n\nSince Peter wants to spend *less than* $\\$320$, the following inequality must be satisfied:\n\n$$\n50d+70 < 320\n$$\n\nSolving for $d$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50d+70 & <320 \\\\ 50d & <320-70 \\\\ 50d & <250 \\\\ d & <\\frac{250}{50} \\\\ d & <5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Peter can rent the van for *less than* $5$ days. So, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186115518 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x \\to  π/2}((2\\sin x - 4)^{2})/(2x)$."] [17592186115754 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)=(\\lim_{t\\to 2}{t}^{2})\\,i+(\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{t-4}{2})\\,j+(\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{1}{t})\\,k\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}{t}^{2} & ={2}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{t-4}{2} & =\\frac{2-4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{1}{t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)=4\\,i-j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k.$"] [17592186115755 :question/problem "Find the limit $\\lim_{t \\to  0}‖f(t)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t) = (t^{2} - 1)i + \\frac{1 - \\cos t}{t}j + \\frac{\\sin t}{t}k.\n$$"] [17592186115757 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t\\to 1}[5f(t)+2g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{3},\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨5-{t}^{3},{t}^{2}-t+2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115757 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 1}({t}^{2}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}({t}^{3}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{1}^{2},{1}^{3},\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}g(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 1}(5-{t}^{3}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}({t}^{2}-t+2),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}(4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5-{1}^{3},{1}^{2}-1+2,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}[5f(t)+2g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 1}5f(t)+\\lim_{t\\to 1}2g(t) \\\\  & =5\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)+2\\lim_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =5⟨1,1,1⟩+2⟨4,2,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,5,5⟩+⟨8,4,8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨13,9,13⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115759 :question/problem "Find the limit $\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{4t}{\\pi }\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115759 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\,(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\,\\left(\\frac{4t}{\\pi }\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  \\\\ 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{\\pi }\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\pi }g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\,(2+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\pi }\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\ (2+\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\pi ) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to \\pi }f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to \\pi }g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115761 :question/worked-solution "A vector-valued function is continuous at a point if and only if each of its components is continuous at that point.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- Function $f(t)$ is **not** continuous at $t=0$ since its first component $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t$ is not continuous at $t=0.$ Indeed, the left and right limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to +0}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=+\\infty  \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to -0}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, $\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t$ doesn’t exist.\n\n- Function $g(t)$ is **not** continuous at $t=0$ since its first component $\\frac{2}{t}$ is not continuous at $t=0.$\n\n- Function $h(t)$ is continuous at $t=0$ because each of its components $2t$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and ${t}^{4}$ are continuous at $t=0.$\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ $h(t)$ only."] [17592186115765 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{s \\to  0}f(s)$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s) = ⟨\\frac{1}{e^{s}}, \\frac{\\sin 3s}{6s}, s^{2} - 1⟩.\n$$"] [17592186115766 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{s \\to  0}‖f(s)‖$, if $f(s) = \\begin{bmatrix}s^{2} + 6 \\\\ \\frac{s}{s - 10} \\\\ e^{5s}\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186115773 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t \\to  1}[f(t) \\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(t)|= ⟨\\sin πt, \\frac{1}{t}, e^{t}⟩; g(t)|= ⟨(\\tan (t - 1))/(t - 1), \\frac{t^{2}}{2 - t}, \\sqrt{t}⟩\n$$"] [17592186115774 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t\\to 0}[3f(t)-g(t)]$ given that\n$f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,{t}^{2}-2,\\frac{t-3}{{t}^{2}+1}⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\sqrt{t}+\\sqrt{2},2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115774 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 0}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}-2),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{t-3}{{t}^{2}+1}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,{0}^{2}-2,\\frac{0-3}{{0}^{2}+1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,-3⟩ \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(\\sqrt{t}+\\sqrt{2}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\sqrt{0}+\\sqrt{2},2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\sqrt{2},2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[3f(t)-g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}3f(t)-\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =3\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)-\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =3⟨1,-2,-3⟩-⟨0,\\sqrt{2},2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-6,-9⟩-⟨0,\\sqrt{2},2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3-0,-6-\\sqrt{2},-9-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-6-\\sqrt{2},-11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115799 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{y \\to  π/3}3$."] [17592186115800 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\sqrt{2}}π$."] [17592186115801 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x \\to  3}x^{4}$."] [17592186115802 :question/problem "Consider a vector-valued function $f(u)$ that is continuous over all real numbers and the real-valued function $u(t) = \\sqrt{9 - t^{2}}$. Then,\n$\\lim_{t \\to  0}f(u(t)) =$"] [17592186115804 :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple, and the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)+\\lim_{t\\to 2}2g(t) \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)+2\\lim_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =f(2)+2g(2) \\\\  & =\\underbrace{⟨1,1,2⟩}_{f(2)}+2g(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)]=⟨-1,7,0⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $g(2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)] & =⟨-1,7,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,2⟩+2g(2) & =⟨-1,7,0⟩ \\\\ 2g(2) & =⟨-1,7,0⟩-⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ 2g(2) & =⟨-2,6,-2⟩ \\\\ g(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ⟨-2,6,-2⟩ \\\\ g(2) & =⟨-1,3,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186115806 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x \\to  - 2}x^{5}$."] [17592186115807 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{y \\to  3}\\frac{5y^{2}}{3}$."] [17592186115808 :question/problem "Given that $\\lim_{x \\to  \\sqrt{2}}Cx^{6} = 8$, where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C$?"] [17592186115809 :question/problem "![](images/c4/c469897cf60d68b90b1e1c0d4a79ca87353735cc76d395ed9a14c42c9a7e54fc.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $y = f(x)$. Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  1}(-6f(x))$."] [17592186115810 :question/problem "![](images/42/42510bb060b0ec667fedc1cff3336c3efaa6cf8c4a11df1599b05a15554ddc4e.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $y = f(x)$. Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\sqrt{7}f(x)$."] [17592186115864 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x \\to  π}2x\\cos (x)$."] [17592186115872 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}2\\sin (x - \\frac{π}{6})$."] [17592186115873 :question/problem "What is $\\lim_{x \\to  (π/9)}\\tan (3x)$?"] [17592186115874 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}\\tan x$."]}
